  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "f08c1619-709b-444c-b57d-a8a4c7e6d95a",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Java 15 Changes\n\n## Language changes\n\n\n### Standard\n\n- Text Block\n\n    - Text Blocks graduate from second-preview (Java 14) to a permanent language feature. \n    - Triple-quoted multi-line strings with automatic incidental-whitespace stripping and the escape sequences introduced in JEP 368.\n\n\n### Preview\n\n- Records\n\n    - Second preview of records (first was Java 14 / JEP 359). \n    - Refinements: local record classes inside methods are now allowed; records can be annotated; generic records are supported. \n    - The compiler continues to auto-generate canonical constructor, accessors, equals(), hashCode(), toString().\n\n```sh\n    # Records are still preview in Java 15 — must opt in\n    javac --enable-preview --release 15 MyClass.java\n    java  --enable-preview MyClass\n\n```\n\n- Sealed classes\n\n    - New language feature (first preview). \n    - A sealed class or interface restricts which other classes/interfaces may extend or implement it, using the permits clause.\n    - Enables exhaustive type hierarchies and works naturally with pattern matching.\n\n- Pattern Matching of `instanceof`\n\n    - Second preview (first was Java 14 / JEP 305).\n    - No spec changes from the first preview — the re-preview collects further feedback. \n    - Binds a pattern variable in the instanceof check, eliminating the explicit cast.\n\n## Platform Changes\n\n### Standard\n\n- ZGC Production Ready\n    - ZGC graduates from experimental (Java 11–14) to a fully supported production GC. \n    - Sub-millisecond pause times, concurrent compaction, and heap sizes from 8 MB to 16 TB.\n    - Available on Linux, macOS (Java 14+), and Windows (Java 14+).\n\n```sh\n\n    # Use ZGC (now fully production-supported)\n    java -XX:+UseZGC -Xmx4g MyApp\n\n    # Soft max heap — ZGC returns memory to OS proactively\n    java -XX:+UseZGC -Xms256m -Xmx8g \\\n        -XX:SoftMaxHeapSize=2g MyApp\n\n    # Log ZGC activity\n    java -XX:+UseZGC \\\n        -Xlog:gc*:gc.log:time,uptime \\\n        MyApp\n\n    # Tune concurrent GC threads\n    java -XX:+UseZGC \\\n        -XX:ConcGCThreads=4 \\\n        MyApp\n\n    # G1 (default) — balanced throughput/latency\n    java -XX:+UseG1GC \\\n        -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200 \\\n        MyApp\n\n    # ZGC — ultra-low latency (< 1 ms pauses)\n    java -XX:+UseZGC MyApp\n\n    # Verify active GC\n    java -XX:+UseZGC \\\n        -Xlog:gc::time MyApp\n    # [2024-...] Using ZGC\n```\n\n- Shenondoah GC\n    - Shenandoah graduates from experimental to fully supported.\n    - Like ZGC, it performs concurrent compaction to keep GC pause times low and independent of heap size.\n    - Open-source; available in OpenJDK builds.\n\n```sh\n\n    # Use Shenandoah (now production-supported)\n    java -XX:+UseShenandoahGC -Xmx4g MyApp\n\n    # Heuristics — adaptive (default), static, compact, aggressive\n    java -XX:+UseShenandoahGC \\\n        -XX:ShenandoahGCHeuristics=adaptive \\\n        MyApp\n\n    # GC logging\n    java -XX:+UseShenandoahGC \\\n        -Xlog:gc*:shenandoah.log:time \\\n        MyApp\n\n    # Note: not available in all JDK distributions\n    # (included in OpenJDK builds; check your vendor)\n```\n\n- Edwards-Curve Digital Singature\n\n    - Adds EdDSA cryptography (RFC 8032) to the JCA.\n    - Ed25519 and Ed448 curves offer better security and performance than RSA or ECDSA at comparable key sizes.\n    - Platform-independent implementation.\n\n- Hidden Classes\n\n    - Introduces hidden classes: classes generated at runtime that cannot be discovered by name, linked by other classes, or accessed via reflection. \n    - Designed as a replacement for Unsafe::defineAnonymousClass, used internally by lambdas and other framework code.\n\n- DatagramSocket API\n\n    - Replaces the underlying implementations of java.net.DatagramSocket and java.net.MulticastSocket with a modern, maintainable implementation — completing the socket reimplementation started by JEP 353 (Java 13) for TCP sockets. \n    - The public API is unchanged.\n\n```java\n\n    import java.net.*;\n\n    // DatagramSocket API — unchanged; only internals replaced\n    try (DatagramSocket socket = new DatagramSocket(9000)) {\n        byte[] buf = new byte[1024];\n        DatagramPacket packet = new DatagramPacket(buf, buf.length);\n\n        socket.receive(packet);  // blocks until datagram arrives\n        String msg = new String(packet.getData(), 0, packet.getLength());\n        System.out.println(\"Received: \" + msg);\n    }\n\n    // MulticastSocket — same API, new internals\n    try (MulticastSocket ms = new MulticastSocket(5000)) {\n        InetAddress group = InetAddress.getByName(\"230.0.0.0\");\n        ms.joinGroup(group);\n        // receive multicast packets as before\n        ms.leaveGroup(group);\n    }\n```\n\n```sh\n\n# If you encounter a regression, roll back to old impl:\njava -Djdk.net.usePlainDatagramSocketImpl=true MyApp\n\n# (This escape hatch will be removed in a future release)\n```\n### Deprecated\n\n\n\n### Removed",
